<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Sioux War of 1876</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Black Hills War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Sioux War of 1876, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a> War, was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the Lakota and Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> after gold was found in the Black Hills. Warriors under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> won at the Rosebud and the Little Bighorn in June 1876, but winter campaigns wore them down. Crazy Horse surrendered in 1877, Sitting Bull fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and the United States annexed the Black Hills.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-sioux-war-of-1876.jpg" alt="Great Sioux War of 1876" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Marion Russell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Marion_Russell_-_The_Custer_Fight_(1903).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fort-laramie-1868">Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868</a>, signed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-clouds-war">Red Cloud's War</a>, set aside the western half of present-day South Dakota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a> included, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-sioux-reservation">Great Sioux Reservation</a>, with the Powder River Country as unceded hunting grounds closed to white settlers. Custer's expedition of 1874 reported gold in the Black Hills, and thousands of miners followed in breach of the treaty. In May 1875 Spotted Tail, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-cloud">Red Cloud</a> and Lone Horn went to Washington to ask President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> to keep the treaty, but refused an offer of $25,000 and removal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In November 1875 Grant, Lieutenant General Philip Sheridan and Brigadier General George Crook agreed that the army would stop evicting miners. The bands outside the reservation were told to report to the agencies by 31 January 1876, in the depth of winter, and on 8 February Sheridan ordered the campaigns to begin. On 17 March Colonel Joseph J. Reynolds burned a village, Northern Cheyenne rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> camp the army believed it was, but withdrew under fire.</p><p>In the summer three columns under Crook, General Alfred Terry and Colonel John Gibbon converged on the hunting grounds. Crook was checked at the Rosebud on 17 June, and on 25 June nearly 270 men of the Seventh Cavalry, Custer among them, were killed at the Little Bighorn.</p><p>The army then changed its tactics. In August Congress cut off the rations of the Sioux until they ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>, and in October troops surrounded the villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-cloud">Red Cloud</a> and Red Leaf at the agencies. Colonel Ranald S. Mackenzie destroyed a Northern Cheyenne village in the Dull Knife Fight on 25 November 1876, and Colonel Nelson A. Miles campaigned through the winter from the Tongue River, fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> at Wolf Mountain in January 1877. Peace missions led by George Sword, Spotted Tail and Red Cloud persuaded band after band to come in.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Agreement of 1877, enacted on 28 February, took the Sioux land. Dull Knife's and Standing Elk's Northern Cheyenne surrendered on 21 April 1877 and were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>; many who broke out of Fort Robinson in January 1878 were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-cloud">Red Cloud</a> on 5 May and was fatally bayoneted at Camp Robinson in September 1877. Most of the Lakota who had gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> surrendered in 1880 and 1881. Nearly two-thirds of the Lakota had stayed at the agencies, and the divisions of the war shaped their politics for decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Sioux War of 1876” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After gold was found in the Black Hills in 1874, the <b>Great Sioux War</b> followed; Lakota and Cheyenne under Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
